    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and the full suite passes with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `membershipNumber` assignment (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`, where `YY` is the last two digits of the registration date year) following the project's existing OfficeFloor function-injection pattern \u2014 the same approach used for `customerCode`, `householdId`, and `namesakeCount`.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New function class** `AssignMembershipNumber.java` \u2014 a single-responsibility step that reads the built owner's `customerCode` and `registrationDate` and sets the derived membership number in place. Placed after `AssignCustomerCode` and `DefaultRegistrationDate` so both inputs are set.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignMembershipNumber` step (governed by `transaction`) into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `assignHousehold`, before save, so the number is stored and returned.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `membershipNumber` field (`@Column(name = \"membership_number\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `membershipNumber` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically.\n\n5. **Schema DDL** (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `membership_number` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe derivation reuses the owner's own persisted fields (no dependency on seed data), matching the test's expectation that `membershipNumber == customerCode + \"-M\" + <last two digits of registration year>`.",
